message:
Now I see the key issue. In `read_text`:

```c
c = *pi->s++;
switch (c) {
case '<':
    done = 1;
    pi->s--;
    break;
case '\0': set_error(&pi->err, "invalid format, document not terminated", pi->str, pi->s); return;
```

When `pi->s = pi->str - 1` (before the buffer), `c = *pi->s++` reads `*(pi->str - 1)`. If `c == '\0'`, then `set_error` is called with `current = pi->str` (after `++`). `xml = pi->str`, `current = pi->str`. No crash.

But what if `c` is NOT `\0`? Then `read_text` continues. It reads `*(pi->str)`, `*(pi->str + 1)`, etc. If it hits `\0` at some position `p`, `set_error` is called with `current = p`. Since `p >= pi->str`, `current >= xml`. No crash.

Hmm, but what if `c` is `<`? Then `done = 1` and `pi->s--` makes `pi->s = pi->str - 1` again. Then after the loop:
```c
*b = '\0';
if (0 != alloc_buf) {
    fix_newlines(alloc_buf);
    pi->pcb->add_text(pi, alloc_buf, ('/' == *(pi->s + 1)));
    xfree(alloc_buf);
} else {
    fix_newlines(buf);
    pi->pcb->add_text(pi, buf, ('/' == *(pi->s + 1)));
}
```

`*(pi->s + 1)` = `*(pi->str)` = `<`. So `'/' == '<'` is false. `add_text` is called.

Then back in `read_element`:
```c
if ('/' == *(pi->s + 1) && ...) {
```

`*(pi->s + 1)` = `*(pi->str)` = `<`. Not `/`. So this is skipped.

Then the while loop continues:
```c
while (!done) {
    start = pi->s;  // start = pi->str - 1
    next_non_white(pi);
    ...
    c = *pi->s++;
    ...
}
```

`start = pi->str - 1`. `next_non_white(pi)` advances `pi->s`. Then `c = *pi->s++` reads the next char.

Wait, but `next_non_white` might not advance `pi->s` if `*pi->s` is not whitespace. Let me check:

<｜｜DSML｜｜=execute_bash>
<｜｜DSML｜｜=command>sed -n '74,90p' /workspace/repo-vul/src-vul/ext/ox/parse.c</｜｜DSML｜｜>
</｜｜DSML｜｜>

